ituation  and uh you know we just you know had  open space in the locker room  to discuss these things whether we was  going to play or not  um similar to the bubble and like you  said it's 2021 and  we kind of continue to find ourselves in  the same situation and  it's tough because you know we're not  surprised by what we see on tv  um you know it's it's just it's the  world we live in  you know i just feel like you know the  same energy should be kept  as you know when you know we see our  people um  you know peacefully protesting for you  know  things that we see uh you know our  people getting  you know murdered on on tv and live  and and videos and you know they're  protesting the capital or riding the  capital  you know for losing an election you know  it's two different things and i want  the same energy on tv you know to be you  know their thugs  and criminals you know the same terms  that they use you know when they see our  people  you know protesting for losing our lives  and uh  you know i think it's bigger than  basketball is bigger than the game today  but i just felt like that  you know the statement us talking about  it us doing on the court on national tv  um could you know shed more light than  not playing and you know that's what we  talked about  gary washburn uh  for either you two guys or both  how do you manifest your anger into  something positive  because it's easy to be pissed you're  everybody you know we're all pissed as  black men  um given what's happened but how do you  kind of  manifest that and turn that into  something positive and productive  especially you guys are role models etc  um  how do you kind of do that that's tough  to be so young  and angry and justifiably angry but then  try to not let the anger body  or get to you it's definitely two sides  to a coin like  um basketball of course is our  profession and we're blessed to be in  this position  but we're also you know men we also our  fathers or  members of our community as well so when  they are we are  and we want to continue to be a voice  for the voiceless um but  you know sports is often used as a way  to lift people up it's a way to spread  joy  and uh that's the decision that we came  out here today with and hopefully we got  the win and we spread some joy today  jared weiss  you guys had these similar discussions  back in the bubble when the jacob bleach  uh blake shooting happens how did going  through that process before help  you come to your decision tonight and  also what made you guys want to come out  here together and speak  and stand together uh i think  you know just being unfortunately being  in a situation in a bubble and knowing  that  you know we had the the talks of you  know cancelling the whole bubble and  leaving  but realizing that you know as the nba  as players and house  the platform that we have on national tv  knowing that everyone's going to be  watching  um and be in front of a camera you know  we can play the game but you know when  we get asked questions we can  you know share light and talk about the  things that we want to express  and i think this is a great way it's  going to have to continue unfortunately  until we see real change  sherrod blakely  yeah uh this question is for both of you  but specifically for for you  jalen uh what we're talking about  happening at the capitol building what  we saw happen with the shooter of jacob  blake  and you contrast that with the elections  in  georgia and how those turned out and how  you guys played a role in  frankly moving people to be more engaged  in a civic  uh prospect of voting how do you  compartmentalize  all those different emotions obviously  the the joy of seeing more people  be participating in the voting process  but still the issues that you see happen  at the capitol building  and obviously jacob blake's a shooter  not being charged how do you  compartmentalize the emotions that all  those things  kind of bring about and create like 